1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,475
Hi everyone,

2
00:00:01,495 --> 00:00:06,848
and welcome back to Sewing Machine Design and Manufacturing at WenQuest.

3
00:00:07,248 --> 00:00:13,708
This is Lecture 23, designing, building and testing the sewing machine.

4
00:00:14,108 --> 00:00:17,622
We've worked out the lockstitch mechanisms one by one,

5
00:00:17,642 --> 00:00:20,960
but right on paper is not yet right in the machine.

6
00:00:20,980 --> 00:00:25,149
Today we follow a lockstitch machine from drawing to production.

7
00:00:25,169 --> 00:00:27,964
How is a gap of tens of microns guaranteed,

8
00:00:27,984 --> 00:00:31,629
and why does a machine start to scream after a speed-up?

9
00:00:33,149 --> 00:00:40,752
A garment factory bought sped-up lockstitch machines:

10
00:00:40,772 --> 00:00:46,649
top speed raised from 5000 to 5500 r/min.

11
00:00:47,049 --> 00:00:51,069
Operators say the machines scream at high speed.

12
00:00:51,469 --> 00:01:00,625
On the test stand, vibration at 5500 is more than three times that at 5000,

13
00:01:00,645 --> 00:01:06,009
and noise rises from 83.5 to about 94 dB(A).

14
00:01:06,409 --> 00:01:10,215
The spectrum shows the second order growing,

15
00:01:10,235 --> 00:01:16,389
and a modal test puts the arm's first bending frequency near 190 hertz.

16
00:01:16,789 --> 00:01:23,348
Why does 10 percent more speed triple the vibration, and how do we fix it?

17
00:01:23,748 --> 00:01:25,088
We'll answer at the end.

18
00:01:26,608 --> 00:01:30,308
A new model goes from launch to production like this.

19
00:01:30,708 --> 00:01:35,501
First the design inputs: the speed, stitch length, fabrics,

20
00:01:35,521 --> 00:01:40,802
noise and price the market wants, and the standards it must meet.

21
00:01:41,202 --> 00:01:45,958
Then concept and detailed design, with process,

22
00:01:45,978 --> 00:01:51,342
quality and service engineers reviewing the drawings.

23
00:01:51,742 --> 00:01:57,207
Then prototypes, tests and redesign, often several rounds;

24
00:01:57,227 --> 00:02:02,691
a pilot batch to prove the tooling and inspection methods;

25
00:02:02,711 --> 00:02:04,866
and finally production.

26
00:02:05,266 --> 00:02:12,337
The most common lesson: the later a problem is found, the more it costs to fix.

27
00:02:12,737 --> 00:02:17,366
So standards and patents are design inputs from day one:

28
00:02:17,386 --> 00:02:23,677
search patents by sales region, design around them, and file your own early.

29
00:02:25,197 --> 00:02:30,726
Tolerance chains: where a gap of microns comes from.

30
00:02:32,246 --> 00:02:40,008
Chapter 5 said the gap between hook point and needle scarf must stay between

31
00:02:40,028 --> 00:02:42,466
0.04 and 0.1 millimetre.

32
00:02:42,866 --> 00:02:49,549
Those tens of microns depend on a long string of part sizes and positions.

33
00:02:49,949 --> 00:02:54,685
On one side: the needle-bar bush bore in the housing,

34
00:02:54,705 --> 00:02:59,979
the needle bar's play in its bush, the needle clamp offset,

35
00:02:59,999 --> 00:03:05,542
the needle's scarf dimension and how it's twisted when fitted.

36
00:03:05,943 --> 00:03:12,476
On the other: the hook shaft bore, the hook point to its mounting datum,

37
00:03:12,496 --> 00:03:15,479
and the hook shaft's radial play.

38
00:03:15,879 --> 00:03:22,139
Eight links end to end, closing on the gap: that's a tolerance chain.

39
00:03:23,659 --> 00:03:28,345
The example gives each of the eight links a tolerance,

40
00:03:28,365 --> 00:03:30,873
around a nominal gap of 0.07.

41
00:03:31,273 --> 00:03:38,788
The most cautious method is worst case: every link at its worst extreme,

42
00:03:38,808 --> 00:03:44,962
so the range is the sum of tolerances, plus or minus 0.153,

43
00:03:44,982 --> 00:03:47,893
far beyond the allowed 0.03.

44
00:03:48,293 --> 00:03:53,767
But all links at their limits at once almost never happens.

45
00:03:54,167 --> 00:04:01,284
The statistical method treats each link as a normal variable with tolerance

46
00:04:01,304 --> 00:04:02,712
at three sigma;

47
00:04:02,732 --> 00:04:09,183
the gap's sigma is the root sum of squares, about 0.0194 millimetre.

48
00:04:09,583 --> 00:04:16,292
A Monte Carlo run of 4000 machines gives about 87 percent good;

49
00:04:16,312 --> 00:04:23,342
about 129 in every thousand either hit the needle or tend to skip.

50
00:04:24,862 --> 00:04:32,082
What industrial lockstitch machines actually do is adjust at assembly.

51
00:04:32,482 --> 00:04:35,554
The hook can slide along its shaft;

52
00:04:35,574 --> 00:04:41,473
the fitter sets the gap to 0.07 against the needle actually fitted,

53
00:04:41,493 --> 00:04:44,742
with a feeler gauge, and tightens up.

54
00:04:45,142 --> 00:04:53,501
On the right, the distribution of 4000 machines cycles through three cases.

55
00:04:53,901 --> 00:04:57,801
Fully interchangeable: big red tails.

56
00:04:58,201 --> 00:05:01,924
Adjusted: the bore positions, clamp offset,

57
00:05:01,944 --> 00:05:08,712
hook dimension and needle links are all absorbed, sigma drops to about 0.0054,

58
00:05:08,732 --> 00:05:10,453
and almost all pass.

59
00:05:10,853 --> 00:05:17,713
But change the needle and its two links come back: about 98 percent.

60
00:05:19,233 --> 00:05:23,733
Adjustment has a catch: it only holds for the needle fitted at the time.

61
00:05:24,133 --> 00:05:29,413
On the floor, needles are changed when they break or blunt,

62
00:05:29,433 --> 00:05:36,509
and a new needle's scarf dimension and twist differ, so those two links return.

63
00:05:36,909 --> 00:05:41,741
To stay good after a change, either demand tighter needle accuracy,

64
00:05:41,761 --> 00:05:46,593
which lands on the needle maker's tolerances, next lecture's topic,

65
00:05:46,613 --> 00:05:49,779
or reset the gap after changing needle size.

66
00:05:50,179 --> 00:05:53,571
That's the real value of chain analysis:

67
00:05:53,591 --> 00:06:00,737
it separates links fixed once by adjustment from links that keep coming back in use.

68
00:06:02,257 --> 00:06:04,397
Assembly, inspection and testing.

69
00:06:05,917 --> 00:06:13,441
Finished machines get vibration tests: by QB/T 1178-2006,

70
00:06:13,461 --> 00:06:21,117
an accelerometer at set points on the head, at set speeds.

71
00:06:21,517 --> 00:06:25,393
More useful than the overall value is the spectrum:

72
00:06:25,413 --> 00:06:31,657
how big the first order at shaft frequency and the second order at twice that are.

73
00:06:32,057 --> 00:06:40,397
On the right, the speed sweeps back and forth from 3000 to 6500.

74
00:06:40,797 --> 00:06:44,821
Blue is the first order, orange the second,

75
00:06:44,841 --> 00:06:49,617
and the grey line is the arm's amplification curve.

76
00:06:50,017 --> 00:06:54,878
The needle bar's inertia force has first- and second-order parts,

77
00:06:54,898 --> 00:06:58,632
and balance weights cancel only part of the first,

78
00:06:58,652 --> 00:07:04,339
so when the second order nears the arm's natural frequency, vibration soars.

79
00:07:05,859 --> 00:07:11,639
Case: why it screams after the speed-up.

80
00:07:13,159 --> 00:07:14,699
Back to our problem.

81
00:07:15,099 --> 00:07:20,597
On the right, the speed sweep, with the dot moving back and forth.

82
00:07:20,997 --> 00:07:28,129
Vibration is almost flat below 5000, rises steeply above it,

83
00:07:28,149 --> 00:07:34,685
peaks near 5710 r/min, and falls again beyond the peak.

84
00:07:35,085 --> 00:07:37,814
The growth is in the second order.

85
00:07:38,214 --> 00:07:46,519
A 10 percent speed rise adds only about 21 percent to the inertia force,

86
00:07:46,539 --> 00:07:54,036
368 to about 445 newtons, yet vibration becomes 3.3 times larger.

87
00:07:54,436 --> 00:07:58,256
What grew isn't the force; it's the amplification.

88
00:07:59,776 --> 00:08:03,883
Treat the arm as a single-degree-of-freedom system:

89
00:08:03,903 --> 00:08:08,254
response equals static deflection times amplification,

90
00:08:08,274 --> 00:08:13,838
which is 1 over the square root of: one minus r squared, all squared,

91
00:08:13,858 --> 00:08:19,908
plus two zeta r, all squared; where r is excitation over natural frequency.

92
00:08:20,308 --> 00:08:36,248
At 5000 r/min the second order is 167 hertz, r is 0.88, amplification 4.2.

93
00:08:36,648 --> 00:08:47,577
At 5500 it's 183 hertz, r is 0.96, amplification about 11.

94
00:08:47,977 --> 00:08:55,477
Ten percent more speed, about 2.6 times the amplification: that's resonance.

95
00:08:56,997 --> 00:08:59,602
There are three kinds of fix.

96
00:09:00,002 --> 00:09:02,689
One, change the structure:

97
00:09:02,709 --> 00:09:10,393
ribs and thicker walls inside the arm raise modal stiffness 30 percent and

98
00:09:10,413 --> 00:09:14,662
the natural frequency to about 217 hertz;

99
00:09:14,682 --> 00:09:20,805
at 5500, vibration drops to about 3.4 millimetres a second.

100
00:09:21,205 --> 00:09:31,804
Two, add damping: a damping ratio of 0.06 only gets to 9.0, and 0.10 to 6.1.

101
00:09:32,204 --> 00:09:36,296
Three, cut the excitation at its source:

102
00:09:36,316 --> 00:09:43,184
20 percent less reciprocating mass gives 10.6, 40 percent less 8.0.

103
00:09:43,584 --> 00:09:50,237
Conclusion: moving the natural frequency out of the working range works best;

104
00:09:50,257 --> 00:09:51,884
the other two help.

105
00:09:53,404 --> 00:09:57,828
Now back to tolerance chains, on a robot.

106
00:09:58,228 --> 00:10:05,517
A robot's tool-centre-point error is a tolerance chain too: link lengths,

107
00:10:05,537 --> 00:10:11,324
joint zeros, gearbox backlash and tool length, end to end.

108
00:10:11,724 --> 00:10:17,693
Making every part precise enough for a tenth of a millimetre is expensive,

109
00:10:17,713 --> 00:10:22,144
so industry does what we did with the hook gap: adjust.

110
00:10:22,544 --> 00:10:28,783
After assembly a laser tracker calibrates the kinematics and the errors go

111
00:10:28,803 --> 00:10:31,574
into the controller's parameters,

112
00:10:31,594 --> 00:10:32,759
absorbed once.

113
00:10:33,159 --> 00:10:38,569
But the calibration holds only for the tool fitted then,

114
00:10:38,589 --> 00:10:42,738
just as the gap holds only for that needle,

115
00:10:42,758 --> 00:10:47,489
so every tool change needs a new TCP calibration.

116
00:10:49,009 --> 00:10:52,101
The most familiar resonance is the washing machine.

117
00:10:52,501 --> 00:10:57,454
As it spins up, at one particular speed it suddenly shudders,

118
00:10:57,474 --> 00:11:01,041
and once it's faster it runs smoothly again.

119
00:11:01,441 --> 00:11:07,873
At that moment the drum's excitation frequency is passing the machine's

120
00:11:07,893 --> 00:11:09,509
natural frequency:

121
00:11:09,529 --> 00:11:10,417
resonance.

122
00:11:10,817 --> 00:11:14,245
Our sewing-machine arm is exactly the same,

123
00:11:14,265 --> 00:11:19,697
except that its operating point happened to sit halfway up the peak.

124
00:11:21,217 --> 00:11:22,517
Back to the start.

125
00:11:22,917 --> 00:11:34,022
At 5000 r/min the second-order excitation is 167 hertz, frequency ratio 0.88,

126
00:11:34,042 --> 00:11:43,558
amplification about 4.2, vibration about 4.0 millimetres a second.

127
00:11:43,958 --> 00:11:52,583
At 5500 it's 183 hertz, ratio 0.96, amplification about 11,

128
00:11:52,603 --> 00:11:59,469
vibration about 13.3, and noise about 94 dB(A).

129
00:11:59,869 --> 00:12:03,911
The inertia force grew only about 21 percent;

130
00:12:03,931 --> 00:12:10,049
the second-order excitation crept up to the arm's natural frequency.

131
00:12:10,449 --> 00:12:11,889
That's resonance.

132
00:12:12,289 --> 00:12:21,313
Ribs that raise stiffness 30 percent lift the natural frequency to about 217 hertz:

133
00:12:21,333 --> 00:12:25,890
vibration about 3.4, noise about 82 dB(A).

134
00:12:27,410 --> 00:12:31,978
Now Virtual lab 23-1, tolerance chains and resonance,

135
00:12:31,998 --> 00:12:37,951
which you can do yourself on WenQuest. Results and tasks are blurred.

136
00:12:37,971 --> 00:12:38,990
On page one,

137
00:12:39,010 --> 00:12:45,309
the left shows the hook-gap distribution of 4000 machines and each link's

138
00:12:45,329 --> 00:12:46,434
contribution;

139
00:12:46,454 --> 00:12:50,070
on the right you set the eight tolerances.

140
00:12:50,470 --> 00:12:55,374
Task one: no adjustment and no needle change.

141
00:12:55,394 --> 00:13:03,362
Move the tolerance sliders to reach a 99 percent yield at no more than 33

142
00:13:03,382 --> 00:13:05,222
yuan per machine.

143
00:13:05,242 --> 00:13:13,210
First ask which links have a big variance share and are cheap to tighten.

144
00:13:13,610 --> 00:13:16,863
Task two: adjust the gap at assembly.

145
00:13:16,883 --> 00:13:21,729
The absorbed links turn grey in the contribution chart.

146
00:13:21,749 --> 00:13:24,914
Do they still need to be so precise?

147
00:13:24,934 --> 00:13:29,868
Reach a 99 percent yield at 28 yuan or less per machine.

148
00:13:30,268 --> 00:13:36,972
Task three: after adjusting, change to a new needle on the shop floor,

149
00:13:36,992 --> 00:13:39,854
see which two links come back,

150
00:13:39,874 --> 00:13:45,328
and find a way back to a 99 percent yield within 30 yuan.

151
00:13:45,728 --> 00:13:49,786
Switch to page two, speed and resonance.

152
00:13:49,806 --> 00:13:57,534
Drag the top speed from 5000 to 5500 and watch the operating point climb the

153
00:13:57,554 --> 00:14:00,388
slope of the resonance peak.

154
00:14:00,788 --> 00:14:03,822
Task four: change the damping ratio,

155
00:14:03,842 --> 00:14:09,167
the reciprocating mass and the arm's natural frequency in turn,

156
00:14:09,187 --> 00:14:15,529
and compare the three fixes to bring vibration and noise into spec at 5500.

157
00:14:15,549 --> 00:14:19,007
The results are blurred; try it yourself.

158
00:14:19,027 --> 00:14:23,248
The guide and report template are on the platform.

159
00:14:24,768 --> 00:14:30,250
The gap set at assembly has to be recorded, machine by machine, in the factory.

160
00:14:30,270 --> 00:14:32,826
This is the WenQuest digital factory.

161
00:14:32,846 --> 00:14:37,144
The adjustment station measures the hook gap on every machine,

162
00:14:37,164 --> 00:14:40,626
the readings go over the bus into a control chart,

163
00:14:40,646 --> 00:14:44,108
and if the data drifts the system raises an alarm.

164
00:14:44,508 --> 00:14:49,935
Switch to the trace page. On the right is the machine record:

165
00:14:49,955 --> 00:14:55,649
each machine's hook batch, hook gap, hook timing and final test,

166
00:14:55,669 --> 00:14:59,400
each one a record in the history database.

167
00:14:59,800 --> 00:15:03,638
Drag the serial number and look at them one by one.

168
00:15:03,658 --> 00:15:09,160
Today's adjustment at assembly and final inspection are done, logged and,

169
00:15:09,180 --> 00:15:14,077
if something goes wrong, traced like this, one machine at a time.

170
00:15:14,097 --> 00:15:18,540
We'll walk through the whole digital factory in Lecture 27.

171
00:15:20,060 --> 00:15:24,552
Your assignment has four questions. One: a gap set by five links.

172
00:15:24,572 --> 00:15:28,925
Find its range and yield by worst case and by statistics; then,

173
00:15:28,945 --> 00:15:34,062
with the first three absorbed by adjustment, find the new sigma and yield,

174
00:15:34,082 --> 00:15:37,602
and say which links still deserve accuracy control.

175
00:15:38,002 --> 00:15:45,488
Two: an arm with a 200 hertz natural frequency and a damping ratio of 0.03,

176
00:15:45,508 --> 00:15:47,789
planned for 5800 r/min.

177
00:15:47,809 --> 00:15:53,393
Find the second-order frequency ratio and amplification,

178
00:15:53,413 --> 00:15:58,097
recompute with ribs that raise it to 250 hertz,

179
00:15:58,117 --> 00:16:05,302
and compare the three kinds of fix by when they work and what they cost.

180
00:16:05,702 --> 00:16:10,869
Three: complete the four tasks of Virtual lab 23-1. Four:

181
00:16:10,889 --> 00:16:14,510
for a new high-speed lockstitch machine,

182
00:16:14,530 --> 00:16:18,514
list the standards and the IP work it needs,

183
00:16:18,534 --> 00:16:22,882
and say at which stage and by whom each is done.

184
00:16:23,282 --> 00:16:28,342
Submit the assignment and the lab report on the WenQuest platform.

185
00:16:29,862 --> 00:16:34,386
There's also a ten-question quiz. For example,

186
00:16:34,406 --> 00:16:40,511
a chain has three links with tolerances of plus or minus 0.03,

187
00:16:40,531 --> 00:16:45,648
0.03 and 0.02 millimetre. By the statistical method,

188
00:16:45,668 --> 00:16:49,402
what is the range of the closing link?

189
00:16:49,802 --> 00:16:53,850
Or, with the shaft at 4800 r/min,

190
00:16:53,870 --> 00:17:03,342
what is the frequency of the second-order needle-bar inertia force, in hertz?

191
00:17:03,742 --> 00:17:08,425
You get three attempts, and the explanations appear as soon as you submit.

192
00:17:09,945 --> 00:17:11,525
Three things to remember.

193
00:17:11,925 --> 00:17:15,447
One: from design inputs to production,

194
00:17:15,467 --> 00:17:19,827
the later a problem is found the more it costs,

195
00:17:19,847 --> 00:17:25,140
and standards and patents are design inputs from day one.

196
00:17:25,540 --> 00:17:29,107
Two: analyse tolerance chains statistically;

197
00:17:29,127 --> 00:17:35,140
adjustment at assembly absorbs most of the error with one adjustable link,

198
00:17:35,160 --> 00:17:38,564
but a needle change brings two links back.

199
00:17:38,964 --> 00:17:44,902
Three: when excitation nears a natural frequency vibration soars;

200
00:17:44,922 --> 00:17:51,777
fix resonance first by changing the structure to move the natural frequency

201
00:17:51,797 --> 00:17:52,968
out of range.

202
00:17:53,368 --> 00:17:59,079
The assignment, quiz and lab report are on WenQuest.

